 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Onigiri are plain steamed rice with salt; sushi rice is seasoned with vinegar, sugar and salt.</li><li>Use Japanese short-grain rice — jasmine or long-grain falls apart.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Why not sushi rice?"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Molds come in sizes; the common triangle one takes about <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"mass\" data-u=\"g\" data-a=\"75\" data-s=\"0\">75 g</span> of rice (13 onigiri from a 3-cup pot) against ~<span class=\"q\" data-k=\"mass\" data-u=\"g\" data-a=\"110\" data-s=\"0\">110 g</span> by hand (9).</li><li>Pressed in a mold, rice comes out denser — push just until you feel resistance.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Mold sizes"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Japanese Cooking 101’s ajitama onigiri: eggs boiled 7–8 minutes, marinated, then shaped in rice seasoned with their marinade.</li><li>Making it a round ball wrapped all over in nori is what makes it a bakudan (“bomb”).</li><li>Each one takes about <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"mass\" data-u=\"g\" data-a=\"150\" data-s=\"0\">150 g</span> of rice — the 3-cup pot makes 4 bakudan and 3 regular onigiri.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Bakudan"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Spicy tuna-mayo, karaage and kimchi + cheese are this site’s picks (JOC suggests leftover karaage).</li><li>The sriracha amount is a starting point — taste it.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "The loud fillings"
  },
  {
   "html": "<p>Just One Cookbook also seasons the rice itself with soy, sugar, sesame oil and salt before shaping, and suggests unagi or teriyaki sauce as glazes.</p>",
   "title_html": "Yaki onigiri, other glazes"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Rice hardens in the fridge.</li><li>Wrap each onigiri in plastic and then a thick towel to keep it cool but not cold, or freeze yaki onigiri individually for up to a month.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Keeping them"
  }
 ],
